<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajsamand Lake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1662">1662</a>–<a href="/years/1676">1676</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajsamand Lake is an artificial lake near Kankroli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, created between about 1662 and 1676 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raj-singh-i">Raj Singh I</a> of Mewar by damming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gomti-river">Gomati</a> River, partly as famine relief. Its marble embankment, with ornate pavilions and steps, carries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> Prashasti, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> history of Mewar carved on 25 stone slabs, among the longest inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajsamand-lake.jpg" alt="Rajsamand Lake" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koustave · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghat_with_inscriptions_paviions_and_Toranas_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Successive rulers built some of the largest reservoirs of pre-modern India: Lake Pichola was enlarged when Udai Singh II founded Udaipur, Raj Singh I created <b>Rajsamand Lake</b> in the 1660s and 1670s, and Jai Singh built Jaisamand Lake in the late seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
